<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  launchd job description for SentryMCPBridge — the user-level agent that
  owns the Mach service `macstat` and MacStatMCP dial. Installed into
  Sentry.app/Contents/Library/LaunchAgents/ and registered by
  `SMAppService.agent(plistName:)` from `MCPEndpointPublisher`, only when the
  user explicitly asks for it in Settings ▸ AI Access.

  THIS FILE IS THE FIX. Before it existed, `AppDelegate` called
  `NSXPCListener(machServiceName: "dev.malekswilam.macstat.xpc")` and no job
  anywhere declared that name, so launchd had nothing to check the app in
  against, the listener silently never received a connection, and every
  `macstat` invocation failed while the app was running perfectly.
  `MacStatKit/MCPBridge/MCPBridgeContract.swift` has the whole story,
  including the measurement showing why the app's own process could never
  vend this name no matter what plist shipped beside it.

  THE FILE NAME IS PART OF THE API. `SMAppService.agent(plistName:)` looks
  this file up by basename inside Contents/Library/LaunchAgents, and launchd
  requires `Label` below to equal that basename minus ".plist". Both come
  from `MCPBridgeNaming.label`, and `MCPBridgeNamingTests` pins the exact
  string so renaming one without the other fails a test rather than producing
  a silently unregistrable agent — which is, precisely, the bug this file
  fixes.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT

  RunAtLoad / KeepAlive
      Both omitted, so both default to false: this agent is launched *on
      demand*, when something connects to its Mach service, and it is allowed
      to exit. `BridgeService` retires the process after five idle minutes,
      and `KeepAlive` would have launchd immediately resurrect the process
      that just retired itself. Nothing is lost by exiting — launchd starts a
      fresh one on the next connection, and Sentry re-publishes its endpoint.

      This also keeps the honest promise Settings makes: setting this up
      "registers one login item and starts no process until something
      actually connects."

  LimitLoadToSessionType
      Omitted. The default (Aqua) is what is wanted — this agent brokers a
      connection to a running GUI app, so it has nothing to do in a
      pre-login or system context, and naming the default here would only
      invite someone to change it.

  StandardOutPath / StandardErrorPath / EnvironmentVariables / Sockets
      A broker should read nothing it does not need. Logging goes to the
      unified log via `os.Logger`, which needs no file this agent would have
      to create and rotate.

  ProgramArguments
      `BundleProgram` is used instead — a path relative to the app bundle,
      which lets the agent move with the app rather than pinning it to an
      absolute /Applications path a user who keeps apps elsewhere would
      break. It takes no arguments, because there is no argument it should
      accept.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>dev.malekswilam.macstat.xpc</string>

	<!-- Relative to Sentry.app. The MacStat target's Copy Files phase puts
	     the built tool at Contents/MacOS/SentryMCPBridge; see project.yml. -->
	<key>BundleProgram</key>
	<string>Contents/MacOS/SentryMCPBridge</string>

	<!-- The name this whole change is about. Must match
	     `MCPBridgeNaming.machService` — and, because that constant is pinned
	     equal to `MacStatXPCServiceName.machService` by test, it is also the
	     unchanged string `macstat` and MacStatMCP have always dialled. No
	     integration config or documented command had to change. -->
	<key>MachServices</key>
	<dict>
		<key>dev.malekswilam.macstat.xpc</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>

	<!-- What macOS shows the user in System Settings ▸ General ▸ Login Items
	     & Extensions. Without it the entry is an unexplained background item;
	     with it, it is attributed to Sentry. It is also the entry the user
	     toggles off to disable command-line access without removing
	     anything — which is why `MCPEndpointPublisher` re-reads the
	     registration state whenever the pane appears rather than caching it
	     for the life of the app. -->
	<key>AssociatedBundleIdentifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>dev.malekswilam.macstat</string>
	</array>

	<!-- Background: this process is idle except for the moment it hands over
	     an endpoint. `Adaptive` would be claiming it has latency-sensitive
	     work to do; it does not. The one call on its critical path is a table
	     lookup, and the client that pays for a cold start
	     (`macstat statusline`) has its own 50 ms budget and a cached fallback
	     for exactly this reason. -->
	<key>ProcessType</key>
	<string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
